<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/9th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Bulgarophygon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/896">896</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-bulgarian-war-of-894-896">Byzantine–Bulgarian war of 894–896</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Bulgarophygon was fought in 896 near the town of that name in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>, now Babaeski in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simeon-i-of-bulgaria">Simeon I of Bulgaria</a> destroyed a Byzantine army after his Pecheneg allies had driven <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>'s allies, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a>, from their lands north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. The peace that followed obliged Byzantium to pay <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> an annual tribute.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-bulgarophygon.jpg" alt="Battle of Bulgarophygon" width="320" height="133" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: from the Middle Ages, unknown · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boulgarofygon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Under Boris I Bulgaria had become Christian, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-preslav">Council of Preslav</a> in 893 replaced Greek with Old Bulgarian as the language of the church and named his third son Simeon as the next prince. Members of Emperor Leo VI's court then moved the market for Bulgarian goods from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessaloniki</a>, where merchants paid higher taxes. When Leo ignored Simeon's protest, Simeon invaded Byzantine Thrace in what has sometimes been called the first commercial war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, defeating an army under Prokopios Krenites and Kourtikios and cutting off the noses of its captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazar</a> guardsmen.</p><p>Leo turned to diplomacy. At the end of 894 the Byzantine navy ferried the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a> across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, and they defeated Simeon in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dobruja">Dobruja</a> and plundered as far as the outskirts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/preslav">Preslav</a>. Simeon stalled the envoy Leo Choirosphaktes while he allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pechenegs">Pechenegs</a>, and in the decisive battle the Magyars suffered a devastating defeat, after which they moved west into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Simeon broke off the talks and invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> again, encouraged by the death of the general Nikephoros Phokas. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> brought all their themes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagma-military">tagmata</a> from the Arab front under the Domestic of the Schools Leo Katakalon. At Boulgarophygon in the summer of 896 his army was routed; the protovestiarios Theodosius, second in command, was killed, and Katakalon escaped with a few survivors. One soldier is said to have left the world to become the ascetic Luke the Stylite. Simeon marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, and according to al-Tabari Leo armed Arab prisoners of war against him before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a> agreed to negotiate.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The peace lasted formally until about Leo VI's death in 912. In exchange for the tribute, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> returned allegedly 120,000 captured Byzantine soldiers and civilians, received the land between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strandzha">Strandzha</a>, and promised not to invade Byzantine territory. Simeon began a building programme in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/preslav">Preslav</a> meant to rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and imposed his authority on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> by recognising Petar Gojnikovic as its ruler. The lesson of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyar</a> invasion served him in 917, when he kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> from allying with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pechenegs">Pechenegs</a> before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-achelous">Battle of Achelous</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Boulgarophygon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Simeon I (893–927) fought Byzantium and its Magyar allies, calling in the Pechenegs, who drove the Magyars west, and won at <b>Bulgarophygon</b> in 896.</blockquote><a href="/empire/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
